+# Checkpoint R1 independent validation - Sol MEDIUM
+
+VERDICT: BLOCK (r1)
+
+Checker: Codex Sol, MEDIUM effort. Maker files were not edited. Validation used the existing isolated worktree at `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/hq57-checkpoint-wt`; only ignored build artifacts and this checker report were created. HEAD remained `06acb7f4f377a21e4cb2f6b3d712de23170cad30` with maker changes limited to `src/llama-context.cpp` and `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp`.
+
+## Numbered checks
+
+1. Explicit finalization before success return - PASS, with a blocked downstream contract. `src/llama-context.cpp:3694-3720` now calls `llama_io_write_device::commit()` inside the `std::exception` boundary and returns 0 for exceptions. The direct server checkpoint callers in `common/common.cpp:2382-2418` immediately convert that 0 into `GGML_ABORT`, so the served caller still terminates rather than handling a failed save.
+
+2. Previous-image preservation and atomic publication - FAIL. Replacement allocation and replacement-copy failures in `COMMIT_ALLOC` preserve the prior image, and the focused Llama dummy run exercised those legs. The broader transaction claim does not hold: `mbufs[buft]` publishes an empty entry during pass 1, `REFRESH` and `REUSE` intentionally overwrite the live image, and the catch path can erase a still-valid image before the first injected copy occurs.
+
+3. Allocation and placement failures are fallible - FAIL. A null return from `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft` is converted to an exception for positive logical byte totals. However, `ggml_tallocr_alloc` aborts at `ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c:80-84` when padded allocation exceeds the live buffer, before its status can be checked. The new `!= GGML_STATUS_SUCCESS` condition cannot catch that exhaustion case.
+
+4. Zero-byte behavior - FAIL. The `total_size > 0` guard accepts a null allocation for an all-zero tensor set, but pass 2 still calls `ggml_backend_tensor_copy` for those tensors. A checker-only probe built against the same CPU libraries created a zero-byte source view plus an unallocated zero-byte destination and fired `ggml-backend.cpp:476: GGML_ASSERT(buf != NULL && "tensor buffer not set")`; process exit was `-1073740791`. A null destination buffer is invalid even when `ggml_nbytes(dst) == 0` because the buffer assertion precedes the size-zero return.
+
+5. Test coverage for the transaction modes - FAIL. Tests A-D cover first/replacement `COMMIT_ALLOC` paths. No leg forces either `COMMIT_REFRESH` or `COMMIT_REUSE`, so the destructive in-place catch behavior is not tested. Test E saves to previously unused storage 3 but checks storage 1 after failure; it neither seeds nor restores storage 3 and therefore cannot detect a mixed or poisoned storage-3 image.
+
+6. Architecture coverage and recurrent-cache assumptions - FAIL. Serial CPU build succeeded. The focused run over generated `llama-dense.gguf` and `llama-moe.gguf` passed 2/2. The same binary over generated `mamba-dense.gguf` failed 0/1 in Test 11: after the initial 536576-byte save, advancing from 40 to 60 tokens did not change the recurrent checkpoint allocation shape, so `LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_ALLOC=1` was not consumed and the alleged failed replacement returned the normal 76-byte host blob. The test is registered over every generated architecture, so this is a concrete suite regression.
+
+7. RED claim against old code - FAIL. The report says applying only the new test to old HEAD makes leg A crash because the allocator failure is injected. Old `src/llama-context.cpp` has no `LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_ALLOC` seam, so the environment variable cannot cause an allocation failure there. Leg A instead completes the first save and fails its `n_first == 0` assertion. The test is RED on old code, but the claimed crash receipt and failure mechanism are invalid.
+
+8. Build and focused correctness run - PASS for compilation and attention models, FAIL overall. Commands and results:
+
+   - `cmake -S . -B build-check-r1 -G Ninja -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=ON -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DGGML_CUDA=OFF -DGGML_VULKAN=OFF -DGGML_HIP=OFF -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00` - configure PASS.
+   - `cmake --build build-check-r1 --target test-save-load-state --parallel 1` - PASS, linked `build-check-r1/bin/test-save-load-state.exe`.
+   - `cmake --build build-check-r1 --target test-llama-archs --parallel 1` - PASS.
+   - Generated only Llama and Mamba dummy models with `test-llama-archs -a llama` and `-a mamba`.
+   - `test-save-load-state.exe --models build-check-r1/tests/test-models-attn` - PASS, 2 passed / 0 failed.
+   - `test-save-load-state.exe --models build-check-r1/tests/test-models-recur` - FAIL, 0 passed / 1 failed at Test 11 replacement allocation.
+   - No inference service, benchmark, latency, or bandwidth measurement was run.
+
+9. Lazy-path probe - FAIL. The change labels placement as fallible without first checking the called allocator's implementation; the better path was to calculate/check padded placement capacity before calling the aborting allocator or use a genuinely fallible placement primitive. The test also chose byte-total change as a universal replacement trigger without checking recurrent storage semantics, despite being wired into an all-architecture fixture. Neither tradeoff was surfaced in the maker report.
+
+10. Canon-mandate skip scan - PASS within the supplied checkpoint scope. The maker report explicitly surfaced the `common/common.cpp` caller repair as outside its ownership and requested the grant. It is still a release blocker because the supplied checkpoint stop condition requires safe failure propagation.
+
+11. Source-coverage scan - PASS for checker evidence. I read `OPUS-CHECKPOINT-REPORT.md`, the complete diffs for both changed files, the full new Test 11 body and registration, the write/read checkpoint implementations, all direct `common_prompt_checkpoint::update_tgt/update_dft` callers found by `git grep`, `llama_memory_recurrent` state sizing/write code, and the relevant GGML allocator/copy implementations. No prior `CHECK-CHECKPOINT-R1.md` existed. The existing `build-check-r1` tree was incomplete and was resumed serially.
+
+12. Decisions journal, fence, attribution, and cross-lane touchpoints - NOT CHECKABLE from the supplied worktree. There is no maker commit, lane prompt, or `DECISIONS.md` in the checkpoint artifact set. The uncommitted path fence matches the report's two-file ownership claim. The declared cross-lane caller touchpoint in `common/common.cpp` is real and remains unresolved.
+
+## Findings
+
+1. `common/common.cpp:2395-2398,2414-2417` | failed save must propagate without process termination | both direct checkpoint update methods turn the new 0 return into `GGML_ABORT`, so an allocation/finalization failure still kills the server caller | confidence: h
+2. `src/llama-context.cpp:3310-3312` | pass 1 must publish nothing and leave `mbufs` untouched | `mbufs[buft]` inserts a default live entry when the storage has no entry; a subsequent allocation exception leaves a poisoned empty entry behind | confidence: h
+3. `src/llama-context.cpp:3367-3377` | placement exhaustion must be fallible | `ggml_tallocr_alloc` calls `GGML_ABORT` on padded-capacity exhaustion at `ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c:80-84`, so the added status check is unreachable for the failure it claims to handle | confidence: h
+4. `src/llama-context.cpp:3398-3418` | preserve the previous valid checkpoint until replacement succeeds | `REFRESH`/`REUSE` mark the live entry damaged before the injected copy check; `COPY=1` erases a byte-identical prior image although no copy occurred, while later in-place failures necessarily destroy the prior image | confidence: h
+5. `src/llama-context.cpp:3350-3360,3395-3410` | zero-byte null allocation must not be copied through | accepting a null allocation for `total_size == 0` still schedules its tensors for copy; the checker probe hard-aborted at `ggml-backend.cpp:476` because the destination buffer is null before the zero-size early return | confidence: h
+6. `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:935-973` | all-architecture regression test must use architecture-valid state transitions | the 40-to-60-token transition does not change Mamba recurrent checkpoint bytes; focused CPU execution returned 76 instead of injected 0 and failed the registered suite | confidence: h
+7. `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:1000-1019` | multi-buffer failure test must inspect the storage being finalized | Test E saves storage 3 but verifies storage 1, and storage 3 has no prior image; the test cannot detect corruption, empty-entry publication, or mixed state in the affected storage | confidence: h
+8. `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:802-810` and `OPUS-CHECKPOINT-REPORT.md` RED claim | RED evidence must exercise the claimed old-code failure | old code has no R46 injection seam, so applying only the test cannot force the claimed allocator crash; it fails because the save succeeds and returns nonzero | confidence: h
+
+## Fixes owed before recheck
+
+1. Make `update_tgt` and `update_dft` return a recoverable result and handle it at every server caller without `GGML_ABORT`.
+2. Replace pass-1 `operator[]` lookups with non-publishing lookups and erase any newly created top-level storage key when a first save fails.
+3. Remove reliance on `ggml_tallocr_alloc` as a fallible call; preflight the exact padded allocation or introduce/use a non-aborting primitive.
+4. Define and implement truthful semantics for `REFRESH`/`REUSE`. At minimum, preserve the prior image when failure is injected before the first copy and add direct tests for both modes.
+5. Skip zero-byte tensor copies or provide a valid zero-size backend buffer before copy.
+6. Make Test 11 architecture-aware or construct replacement/refresh/reuse states by measured checkpoint shape. Re-run at least one attention, one recurrent, and one hybrid dummy model.
+7. Seed and restore the same storage id in Test E, and correct the old-code RED claim with an actual old-code failure mechanism or state only the assertion failure that can be reproduced.
+
+Cross-lane: `common/common.cpp` is the unresolved caller-repair lane. No other changed-file conflict was found.
