+# Checkpoint R2 independent validation — Sol LOW
+
+VERDICT: BLOCK (r2)
+
+Checker: Codex Sol, LOW effort. I did not make the work under review and did not edit maker code. Validation used `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/hq57-checkpoint-wt` at HEAD `06acb7f4f377a21e4cb2f6b3d712de23170cad30`. No model server, benchmark, performance, latency, or bandwidth run was performed.
+
+1. No mutation during fallible preparation — PASS — `src/llama-context.cpp:3280-3356` constructs contexts, buffers, and copies in local `mbufs_new`; the only live-map operation in `commit()` is the final move assignment. `state_seq_get_data` records whether `mem_storage[key]` was newly created and erases that key in the exception path (`:3647-3684`).
+
+2. Off-map atomic replacement and preservation — PASS — every replacement allocates and copies into `mbufs_new`; `mbufs = std::move(mbufs_new)` publishes only after all allocation and copy legs complete (`src/llama-context.cpp:3319-3356`). The old map remains live through both injected replacement failures. Serial Test 11 runs restored the seeded image after allocation and copy failures on Llama dense, Llama MoE, Mamba, and Jamba.
+
+3. No aborting allocator/placement/destructor or null zero-byte copy — PASS for the required paths — the former `ggml_tallocr_alloc` path is deleted, the destructor is defaulted, positive-size groups use the null-reporting `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft`, and write/read both skip `size == 0` before buffer-type lookup, tensor creation, and copying (`src/llama-context.cpp:3249,3278-3318,3327-3352,3408-3444`). Test 11 leg G round-tripped the 68-byte host header with no device tensor/buffer on every serial architecture run.
+
+4. Caller failure propagation — PASS by full caller scan and compilation — `common_prompt_checkpoint::update_tgt/update_dft` return `[[nodiscard]] bool`, clear data and flags on failure, and contain no abort (`common/common.h:1258-1269`, `common/common.cpp:2385-2442`). `git grep -n -E '\\.update_(tgt|dft)\\(' -- '*.cpp' '*.h'` found nine call sites; all nine inspect the result in `server-context.cpp`, `speculative-simple.cpp`, and `test-recurrent-state-rollback.cpp`. The serial affected-target build exited 0. Runtime server fallbacks remain source-verified only, as serving was out of scope.
+
+5. Exact storage IDs — PASS — Test 11 defines storage IDs 1 through 4 explicitly. Storage 3 is seeded, controlled, failed, and restored using `fl3` throughout (`tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:866,1138-1173`); storage 2 retry and storage-1 independence are also checked. `test-ctx-checkpoint-storage.exe` exited 0 with `PASS (0 failures)`.
+
+6. Architecture-independent coverage — PASS — the replacement failure no longer depends on a checkpoint-size change; every save allocates a new image. Independent serial runs produced `2 passed, 0 failed` for Llama dense + MoE, `1 passed, 0 failed` for Mamba, and `1 passed, 0 failed` for Jamba. Test 11 fired allocation and copy failures in all four models. The two-buffer-type leg remains explicitly unexercised on CPU and is not counted as coverage.
+
+7. Honest RED/negative evidence — PASS — `OPUS-CHECKPOINT-R2-REPORT.md:80-83` explicitly retracts the false R1 historical crash claim: old code had no injection seam, and no old-code allocator crash is claimed. Current-code negative controls fired deterministic allocation/copy failures and returned 0; clean paired saves completed and restored their controls in every focused serial run.
+
+8. Release seam guard — FAIL — `src/CMakeLists.txt:65-67` enables `LLAMA_TEST_FAULT_INJECTION` whenever `LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV` is ON, while root `CMakeLists.txt:14` defaults that option ON. Therefore an ordinary source-built `llama-server` includes live `LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_ALLOC` and `_COPY` environment controls unless the builder remembers an opt-out. This does not meet the prompt's requirement for a seam that cannot be accidentally enabled on a served release. The `LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV=OFF` control is real: with both variables set, `build-rel-noinject/bin/test-save-load-state.exe` skipped Test 11 and passed Mamba 1/1. It proves the opt-out works, not that served builds are protected by default.
+
+9. Build and focused execution — PASS — `cmake --build build-check-r1 --target llama test-save-load-state test-recurrent-state-rollback test-ctx-checkpoint-storage llama-server llama-speculative-simple --parallel 1` exited 0. The serial architecture runs above all exited 0. `git diff --check` exited 0. An initial concurrent checker invocation cross-contaminated the suite's shared `dump_state.bin`; it was discarded and every model set was rerun serially, matching the mandated execution mode.
+
+10. Lazy-path probe — FAIL — reusing the broad, default-ON version-label option was easier than adding a dedicated, default-OFF fault-injection option or another guard that cannot reach normal server builds. The report surfaces the reach honestly, but leaves the explicit seam-safety requirement unsatisfied.
+
+11. Canon-mandate skip scan — PASS — all eight R2 prompt mandates were implemented or explicitly surfaced; only mandate 8 remains technically unsatisfied and is the blocking finding below. CPU cannot exercise a second buffer type, and the report names that limit rather than counting it as coverage.
+
+12. Source-coverage scan — PASS — read in full: `CHECK-CHECKPOINT-R1-SOL.md`, `OPUS-CHECKPOINT-R2-PROMPT.md`, `OPUS-CHECKPOINT-R2-REPORT.md`, both full changed implementation/test diffs, all changed caller diffs, all `update_tgt/update_dft` callers, the relevant allocator/copy definitions, and the build/run outputs. The longest cited source tail did not alter the verdict.
+
+13. Decisions journal, commit fence, and attribution — NOT CHECKABLE for commit acceptance: the checkpoint is intentionally uncommitted and has no `DECISIONS.md`. The eight modified paths match the R2 report; two forced signature-change paths outside the initial ownership list are explicitly disclosed. No Vulkan allocator/policy/kernel path is modified.
+
+FINDINGS:
+
+1. `CMakeLists.txt:14; src/CMakeLists.txt:65-67` | fault-injection seam cannot be accidentally enabled on a served release | the guard uses `LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV`, which defaults ON, so ordinary source-built server binaries accept environment variables that force checkpoint allocation/copy failure; only an explicit `-DLLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV=OFF` disables them | confidence: h
+
+Residual fix owed: gate `LLAMA_TEST_FAULT_INJECTION` behind a dedicated default-OFF test/fault-injection option, or an equally strong mechanism that excludes normal served builds by default. Rebuild the dev test target with the seam explicitly enabled, rebuild a default server configuration without it, and prove the variables fire only in the former.
+
+Cross-lane: no undeclared code touchpoint found. The uncommitted checker reports are artifacts only.
+
