+# Checkpoint finalization safety — R2 maker report
+
+Maker: Claude Opus 5. Worktree `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/hq57-checkpoint-wt`, branch
+`codex/hq57-checkpoint-finalize`, HEAD `06acb7f4f377a21e4cb2f6b3d712de23170cad30`. Nothing committed.
+No inference service, benchmark, latency or bandwidth measurement was run, no scheduled task was
+touched, and no Vulkan allocator policy or kernel was modified.
+
+Files changed:
+
+| File | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `src/llama-context.cpp` | the finalization rewrite, the restore boundary, the seam guard |
+| `src/CMakeLists.txt` | ties the fault-injection seam to `LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV` |
+| `common/common.h`, `common/common.cpp` | `update_tgt`/`update_dft` return a status instead of aborting |
+| `tools/server/server-context.cpp` | the four checkpoint-creation call sites handle that status |
+| `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp` | Test 11 rewritten |
+| `tests/test-recurrent-state-rollback.cpp` | **outside the lane's named ownership** — two call sites forced by the signature change |
+| `examples/speculative-simple/speculative-simple.cpp` | **outside the lane's named ownership** — two call sites forced by the signature change |
+
+A repo-wide grep for `update_tgt|update_dft` found call sites in `examples/` and `tests/` that the
+lane's file list did not name. `[[nodiscard]]` produces a *warning*, not an error, and
+`LLAMA_FATAL_WARNINGS` is OFF, so leaving them would have compiled silently while a caller continued
+past a failed save. Both were given the same fallback as their server counterpart. Flagging the
+out-of-lane touch rather than leaving a latent unhandled failure.
+
+`git diff --check` is clean (exit 0).
+
+## The central change
+
+The previous design had three finalization modes. `COMMIT_ALLOC` allocated a new buffer while the
+old image was still held, but `COMMIT_REFRESH` and `COMMIT_REUSE` wrote into the live buffer, so
+they could not fail without destroying the image they were replacing, and `COMMIT_REUSE` reached
+`ggml_tallocr_alloc`, which calls `GGML_ABORT` on capacity exhaustion (`ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c:80-84`)
+before any status can be inspected.
+
+Those two modes are **deleted**. Every save now builds the entire replacement in a local
+`mbufs_new`: its own contexts, its own buffers, its own tensors. Every allocation and every copy
+happens there. The live map is replaced by a single `mbufs = std::move(mbufs_new)` only after all of
+them have succeeded. One consequence worth stating plainly, because Sol's fix list asked for direct
+tests of both modes: those tests do not exist because the modes do not exist.
+
+This removes the only aborting allocator call from the path. `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft`
+reports failure by returning NULL, and it is now the only allocating call in `commit()`. `grep` for
+`ggml_tallocr` in `src/llama-context.cpp` returns one hit, and it is inside a comment.
+
+## Each Sol finding
+
+**1 — no map mutation in the fallible prepare phase.** `mbufs[buft]` is gone from `commit()`; the
+function never touches the live map before the final move. The top-level `mem_storage[key]` in
+`state_seq_get_data` does still create the entry, so the function now records whether the key was
+new and erases it again in the catch. A first save that fails leaves no entry, and the restore path
+reports that as a failure rather than asserting.
+
+**2 — the previous checkpoint survives byte-for-byte.** Nothing is written into a live buffer, at
+any point, in any mode. The cost is accepted and explicit: a replacement holds the old and the new
+device image at the same time, and every save allocates a fresh buffer even when nothing changed.
+See "peak memory" below.
+
+**3 — no aborting allocation or placement.** Covered by the deletion above. Every injected
+allocation, placement and copy failure now returns 0 through the save boundary. Both destructors are
+`= default`; finalization is an explicit `commit()` on the success path only.
+
+**4 — zero-byte groups.** Write and read both skip `size == 0` entries, in the counting loop and the
+tensor-building loop, so `n_tensors == org.size()` holds on both sides. That matters: the read side
+gates on `mbuf_cur.n_tensors == mbuf.n_tensors` and then indexes `mbuf.org[i]`, so counting an entry
+the vector skips would be an out-of-bounds read. The defined representation of an all-zero group is
+no entry at all — nothing allocated, nothing copied, nothing published — and the restore is
+symmetric, so it round-trips. Test leg G exercises the end-to-end case.
+
+**5 — save failure propagates without `GGML_ABORT`.** `update_tgt` and `update_dft` are now
+`[[nodiscard]] bool`. Both clear their destination on failure, so `empty()` stays truthful and no
+later `load_tgt`/`try_load_dft` can restore a half or stale image. A zero-byte size is treated as a
+failure too, otherwise `n != ckpt_size` would be satisfied by `0 != 0` and report a false success.
+Caller behaviour is in its own section below.
+
+**6 — architecture independence.** No leg depends on a state transition changing the checkpoint byte
+total any more. Because every save allocates, injected attempt 1 exists on every save on every
+architecture. Verified on dense, MoE, Mamba and Jamba; the Mamba regression Sol found is gone.
+
+**7 — the R1 RED claim.** Sol is right and the R1 report was wrong. The pre-R46 code has no
+`LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_ALLOC` seam, so the test cannot force an allocator failure there and the
+claimed historical allocator crash was never executed. I did not execute it either and I do not
+claim it. What the suite proves is stated below under "negative seam".
+
+**8 — production reachability of the seam.** Addressed with a compile guard; see "the seam" below.
+
+## One additional defect found and fixed
+
+Sol's fix list did not name this and the R1 tests could not see it. Running the new leg on the
+recurrent model failed with `restoring the failed replacement's header produced a mixed image`.
+
+The serialized header and the host-side cell metadata are written into the caller's output buffer
+*before* `commit()` runs. When the finalization then fails, that buffer still holds a well-formed
+blob. On a recurrent cache the checkpoint byte total does not change between the two states, so the
+size guard accepts it, and restoring it pairs the failed save's host-side metadata with the previous
+save's device data. That is a genuine mixed-generation restore, and it is reachable by any caller
+that ignores the 0.
+
+`state_seq_get_data` now scrubs the output buffer in its error path. The only thing a caller that
+ignores the 0 can now do is fail the magic check. The three legs that restore a failed save's output
+buffer all assert a 0 return, on every architecture tested.
+
+Related, and also reachable from the failure flow: the restore-side buffer-size mismatch was a
+`GGML_ABORT`. A caller holding a failed replacement's header would have hit it. It is now a thrown
+`std::runtime_error` with the same two totals in the log, so it returns 0.
+
+## Caller behaviour
+
+| Call site | On failure | Provenance |
+|---|---|---|
+| `server-context.cpp:2900` `create_checkpoint` | warns, `checkpoints.pop_back()`, returns — the entry just emplaced is dropped, never retried | CHECKED (compile) / ASSUMED (runtime) |
+| `server-context.cpp:3581` speculative `update_dft` | warns, clears the drafter sequence so the next draft re-prefills | ASSUMED |
+| `server-context.cpp:3650` speculative `update_tgt` | warns, clears the checkpoint, the draft and the dists, returns | ASSUMED |
+| `server-context.cpp:3686` speculative `update_dft` | same as 3581 | ASSUMED |
+| `speculative-simple.cpp:179, 207` | same two fallbacks | ASSUMED |
+| `test-recurrent-state-rollback.cpp:291, 335` | reports and returns 1 | CHECKED (compile) |
+
+**The ASSUMED rows were never executed.** Serving and inference were out of scope for this lane, so
+every server and example call site is compile-checked only. Two links in particular rest on reading
+the code, not on a run: that clearing `spec_draft` without a matching `common_speculative_accept`
+leaves the drafter's internal position consistent, and that `spec_i_batch` is still empty when the
+next pass reaches `GGML_ASSERT(slot.spec_i_batch.empty())` at `server-context.cpp:3569`. The second
+one follows from the batch only being populated at `server-context.cpp:809-811`, which is after this
+point — but that is an argument, not a receipt.
+
+The speculative `update_tgt` case is the one that mattered most. Clearing only `data_tgt` would not
+have been safe: `load_tgt` returns early on empty data, so a partial draft acceptance would have
+silently skipped its rollback and left the sequence holding rejected tokens. Dropping the draft
+instead means this turn decodes the sampled token normally, and the next turn takes the "no previous
+draft" branch at `server-context.cpp:3562` and builds a fresh checkpoint. That branch asserts
+`!spec_ckpt.empty()` only when a previous draft exists, which is exactly what the clear prevents.
+
+No retry loop anywhere. No call site reports success on a failed save.
+
+Three residuals, disclosed:
+
+1. After `update_tgt` succeeds and `update_dft` then fails, `create_checkpoint` drops the list entry,
+   but the target device image `update_tgt` already published stays resident in `mem_storage` until
+   that storage id is reused by the next successful checkpoint. It is unreachable (no list entry
+   refers to it) but it is not freed at that moment.
+2. `create_checkpoint` runs its eviction loop and its `n_ctx_checkpoints` trim *before* the save. A
+   failed save therefore leaves the slot with one fewer restorable checkpoint than it had before the
+   call. That is a consequence of dropping the incomplete entry, and it is graceful degradation
+   rather than corruption, but it is a real cost of the fix.
+3. `common_prompt_checkpoint_update` now also resets `flags_out` to 0 on failure, and `clear()` now
+   resets both flag fields. Without that, `on_device()` could return true on an empty checkpoint;
+   `server-task.cpp:1784` and `server-ckpt-storage.h:44` both read the flags without first checking
+   `empty()`, and a stale ON_DEVICE bit would have kept a storage id reserved for an image that does
+   not exist.
+
+## The seam
+
+`src/CMakeLists.txt` defines `LLAMA_TEST_FAULT_INJECTION` on the `llama` target only when
+`LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV` is ON. Without it, `llama_io_ckpt_fail_at` compiles to a body that increments
+the counter and returns false, with no `getenv` at all, so no environment variable can inject a
+checkpoint failure. `LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV` is the project's existing release switch: `CMakeLists.txt:12`
+says to set it OFF when building from a release tag.
+
+Two things to be accurate about. First, it defaults ON, so the seam is compiled into every build that
+does not explicitly turn it off — that is the guard's real reach, and it is narrower than "test
+builds only". Second, the definition is PUBLIC so the test target sees the same macro; Test 11 skips
+with a message rather than failing when the seam is absent, and the library and the test therefore
+cannot disagree about whether it exists.
+
+The pre-existing `LLAMA_R45_RED_QUANT_VIEW` seam in the same file is the identical hazard class and
+is still unguarded. I left it alone: it is outside this lane and an existing R45 test may depend on
+it being live.
+
+Verified, not assumed. A second isolated build was configured with `-DLLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV=OFF` and the
+suite was then run with **both** injection variables exported:
+
+```
+cmake -S . -B build-rel-noinject -G Ninja -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=ON -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF \
+      -DGGML_CUDA=OFF -DGGML_VULKAN=OFF -DGGML_HIP=OFF -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
+      -DLLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV=OFF -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00
+cmake --build build-rel-noinject --target test-save-load-state --parallel 1
+LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_ALLOC=1 LLAMA_R46_CKPT_FAIL_COPY=1 \
+  test-save-load-state --models build-check-r1/tests/test-models-recur
+```
+
+Result: `SKIPPED: built without LLAMA_TEST_FAULT_INJECTION (LLAMA_BUILD_IS_DEV=OFF), the checkpoint
+failure seam is not compiled in`, then `summary: 1 passed, 0 failed (of 1)`. No
+`failed to allocate` and no `failed to copy` line appeared anywhere in that run — the variables were
+set and had no effect.
+
+## Test coverage
+
+Test 11 legs, all of them seeding and verifying the exact storage id being failed, each against its
+own control taken from a clean restore of its own image:
+
+| Leg | What it forces |
+|---|---|
+| A | first allocation fails on storage 1: returns 0, publishes nothing, restoring the output buffer returns 0, clean control succeeds |
+| B | replacement allocation fails on storage 1: returns 0, restoring the output buffer returns 0, storage 1 still restores to its state-A control |
+| C | replacement copy fails on storage 1: same three assertions, then a clean replacement is verified to actually publish state B |
+| D | first copy fails on storage 2: returns 0, restoring its output buffer returns 0, retry succeeds and reproduces state A, storage 1 still holds its own state-B image |
+| E | storage 3 (`PARTIAL_ONLY`) seeded, controlled, failed and restored — the same storage id throughout |
+| F | two buffer types in one finalization (`FAIL_ALLOC=2`) |
+| G | zero-byte device image: empty sequence, no buffer allocated, round trip succeeds, blob asserted non-empty first so the leg cannot pass on `0 == 0` |
+
+Leg F is **not covered** on a CPU-only build. The log says so explicitly rather than counting it as a
+pass: `multi-buffer leg NOT EXERCISED - this device exposes a single checkpoint buffer type, so
+attempt 2 never happens.` Covering it needs a backend that exposes two checkpoint buffer types, for
+example a partly offloaded cache.
+
+### Negative seam, and its paired clean controls
+
+The seam is live in the current code and the failures are deterministic. From `run-attn.txt`:
+
+```
+E commit: failed to allocate 'CPU' checkpoint buffer (81920 bytes, 4 tensors)
+E state_seq_get_data: error saving state: failed to allocate device checkpoint buffer
+E state_seq_set_data: error loading state: wrong sequence state magic
+E commit: failed to copy checkpoint tensor 0 of 4 into the 'CPU' buffer
+E state_seq_get_data: error saving state: failed to copy device checkpoint tensors
+```
+
+Every one of those legs is paired with a control that runs the identical save with no variable set
+and asserts it succeeds and reproduces the expected state — the "clean control" saves in legs A, C
+and D. That pairing is the evidence, not a claim about old code.
+
+### Zero-byte leg receipt
+
+From `run-recur-v.txt` line 1395, with no `allocated 'CPU' buffer` line between it and the preceding
+leg:
+
+```
+test_ckpt_finalize_failure: the zero-byte device image round-tripped (68 header bytes, no device buffer)
+```
+
+Honest limit: this exercises the case where a save produces no device tensor at all. A group that
+mixes zero-size and non-zero-size writes cannot be forced through the public API, so the `size == 0`
+skip is defensive for that case. The hazard it defends against is real and was demonstrated
+independently by the checker's `zero-copy-probe`, which still aborts at
+`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:476: GGML_ASSERT(buf != NULL && "tensor buffer not set")` when a zero-byte
+copy is issued against a null-backed destination.
+
+## Peak memory tradeoff
+
+Measured allocation sizes, from the `commit: allocated '%s' buffer %.3f MiB` lines:
+
+| Model | Checkpoint buffer |
+|---|---|
+| llama-dense / llama-moe, 40 cells | 81920 B |
+| llama-dense / llama-moe, 60 cells | 122880 B |
+| mamba-dense | 536576 B (0.512 MiB) |
+
+During a replacement both the old and the new buffer are live, so the transient peak for the buffer
+types being replaced is old + new. On the dummy models that is under 1.1 MiB; on a production cache
+it is one full checkpoint image's worth of extra device memory per replacement, per storage id being
+replaced.
+
+The steady-state cost is the second half of it, and it is not transient: `COMMIT_REFRESH` used to
+reuse the existing buffer when the shape was unchanged, which is the common server case. That path
+is gone, so every checkpoint save now allocates and frees a device buffer. Repeated
+allocate-and-free of large device buffers is also a fragmentation risk on backends that do not
+sub-allocate. Neither was measured — no benchmark or latency run was in scope.
+
+The preserved-image guarantee cannot be had without this. An in-place write cannot both fail safely
+and leave the previous bytes intact.
+
