+# R46 phase 1.2 — checkpoint fallible finalization safety
+
+2026-09-12 · Maker: Opus 5 MEDIUM, worktree `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/hq57-checkpoint-wt`, branch
+`codex/hq57-checkpoint-finalize`, inherited from R46b `06acb7f4f377a21e4cb2f6b3d712de23170cad30`
+(verified with `git rev-parse HEAD`; the tree was clean at start). No commits, no builds, no model
+launches, no measurements, no background children, no backup changes. Codex HQ owns integration.
+
+**Scope claim: this repairs a checkpoint-finalization safety defect only. It is NOT a claim about
+the 156893184-byte `ErrorOutOfDeviceMemory` seen in the R46b served logs.** No allocation ID, call
+site or owner receipt ties that failure to this code path, and none was produced here.
+
+## What was wrong (source-level)
+
+`llama_io_write_device` (src/llama-context.cpp) deferred every device tensor write to its
+DESTRUCTOR. In the pre-change file:
+
+* Old line 3294: `mbuf_cur.buf.reset(ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft(...))` stored the
+  allocator result with no check. `ggml-alloc.h:79` documents NULL as the failure answer. (The
+  recovery plan cites line 3290 for this; on the inherited HEAD the unchecked store is at 3294 and
+  3290 is the enclosing `if (need_alloc)`. Same defect, four lines down.)
+* Old line 3292 moved the new bookkeeping over the live entry BEFORE that allocation ran, so the
+  previous valid checkpoint was already destroyed when the allocation was attempted.
+* Old line 3317 then called `ggml_backend_tensor_copy` on tensors whose `buffer` was NULL.
+  `ggml_backend_buffer_get_type` asserts on that (ggml-backend.cpp:330) — the `ggml-backend.cpp:330`
+  assertion named in the checker evidence.
+* Old lines 3308-3309 ignored the `ggml_status` returned by `ggml_backend_view_init` and
+  `ggml_tallocr_alloc` on the buffer-reuse branch.
+* `state_seq_get_data` (old line 3592) returned the byte count from `state_seq_write_data` while the
+  `unique_ptr<llama_io_write_i>` was still alive. Destruction, and therefore all of the above,
+  happened AFTER the caller had been told the save succeeded. The surrounding try/catch could not
+  see it: a throw from a destructor during normal return calls `std::terminate`.
+
+The read side already had the correct shape. R45 (lane-229) moved the read-side validate-and-copy
+out of the destructor into an explicit `commit()` that `state_seq_set_data` calls only after a clean
+deserialization. The write side was never given the same treatment. This change is that symmetry.
+
+## What changed
+
+Owned files edited: `src/llama-context.cpp`, `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp`. Nothing else.
+`src/llama-context.h` needed no change (the class is file-local). `tests/CMakeLists.txt` needed no
+change (Test 11 lands in the already-registered `test-save-load-state` target).
+`tools/server/server-context.cpp` was inspected and NOT edited — see the ceiling below.
+
+| Site | Change |
+|---|---|
+| src/llama-context.cpp:3226-3234 | `llama_io_ckpt_fail_at()` — env-gated deterministic failure injection, same seam style as the existing `LLAMA_R45_RED_QUANT_VIEW` getenv at :3219 |
+| src/llama-context.cpp:3242 | `~llama_io_write_device() = default` — cleanup only, noexcept, never copies, never throws |
+| src/llama-context.cpp:3255-3432 | `commit()` — the old destructor body, restructured into a three-pass transaction |
+| src/llama-context.cpp:3694-3720 | `state_seq_get_data` holds a non-owning `llama_io_write_device *` and calls `commit()` inside the existing try, before returning `n_written` |
+| tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:809-1042 | Test 11 `test_ckpt_finalize_failure` plus the `ckpt_fail_env` helper |
+| tests/test-save-load-state.cpp:1142-1145 | Test 11 registered in `run_save_load_tests_for_model` |
+
+### The transaction
+
+`commit()` classifies each buffer type into one of three modes, then runs three passes:
+
+1. **Pass 1 — every fallible step, publishing nothing.** `COMMIT_ALLOC` (no live buffer, or a
+   different byte total) allocates the new buffer from the NEW context while the previous entry is
+   still held, and throws with the buffer-type name and requested bytes if the result is NULL and
+   `total_size > 0`. The `total_size > 0` guard matters: `ggml-alloc.h:79` also returns NULL when
+   there is nothing to allocate. `COMMIT_REUSE` (same byte total, different chunking) places tensors
+   into the LIVE buffer through `ggml_tallocr`, now checking both `ggml_backend_view_init` and
+   `ggml_tallocr_alloc` statuses. Placement writes no tensor data, so the previous image is still
+   readable at that point. `mbufs` is not mutated anywhere in pass 1.
+2. **Pass 2 — the copies.** `COMMIT_ALLOC` copies into the new buffers, leaving the previous image
+   untouched. `COMMIT_REFRESH` and `COMMIT_REUSE` write in place over the previous image; those
+   buffer types are recorded in `damaged` before the first copy, and a failure erases exactly those
+   entries from `mbufs` rather than leaving a half-written or mixed-generation image behind.
+3. **Pass 3 — publish.** Only moves, no fallible call between them. Publishing per buffer type
+   inside pass 1 would have been a real defect on a multi-buffer-type device: a failure on the
+   second type would leave the first already carrying the new image and the second the old one, and
+   the read-side total-size guard would accept that mixed pair.
+
+Failure semantics, as delivered:
+
+| Failure point | `mbufs` | Caller |
+|---|---|---|
+| Allocation (first, replacement, or any buffer type) | untouched; previous image byte-identical | `state_seq_get_data` returns 0 |
+| Copy into freshly allocated buffers | untouched; previous image byte-identical | returns 0 |
+| Copy already begun in place (`REFRESH`/`REUSE`) | those entries erased | returns 0 |
+| Everything succeeded | new image published atomically | returns the byte count |
+
+No partial allocation leaks: the new buffer lives in a local `ggml_backend_buffer_ptr` until pass 3,
+so a throw frees it. Retry is safe: a failed save leaves either the old entry or no entry, and the
+next call rebuilds from `winfos` with no residue. The serialized host blob format, the flag bits and
+every public API signature are unchanged; the success path performs the same allocations, the same
+`ggml_backend_tensor_copy` calls, in the same order, as before.
+
+### Old-state ownership, inspected before designing
+
+`mbufs` is a reference to `llama_context::mem_storage[mem_storage_key(seq_id, flags)]`
+(src/llama-context.h:388-395); the key is `(storage slot << 32) | seq_id`, so each storage id of each
+sequence owns an independent `llama_memory_buffers` map. `llama_memory_buffer` (llama-context.h:28-38)
+owns `buf` (`ggml_backend_buffer_ptr`) and `ctx` (`ggml_context_ptr`); `org` holds views of the live
+KV tensors and `cpy` holds the checkpoint copies allocated inside `buf`. Overwriting the map entry
+frees the old buffer, which is why the old ordering lost the previous checkpoint before it knew
+whether the replacement could be allocated. R45's storage-id ownership invariant is untouched: this
+change never chooses, shares or reuses a storage id, and `mem_storage_key` is unmodified.
+
+## Refutations and ceilings — read these
+
+1. **The plan's caller-repair file is wrong.** ASTRA-R46-RECOVERY-PLAN.md phase 1 item 2 names
+   `tools/server/server-context.cpp`. The server does not call the state API directly for context
+   checkpoints. `create_checkpoint` (server-context.cpp:2896-2897) calls
+   `common_prompt_checkpoint::update_tgt` / `update_dft`, and those live in **common/common.cpp**
+   (:2382-2418). Both do `if (n != ckpt_size) GGML_ABORT("checkpoint size mismatch: ...")`. So a
+   failed device save now aborts the server at that check. That is strictly better than the
+   pre-change behaviour — no copy into null-backed tensors, no success reported for a checkpoint
+   that does not exist, and the abort message is truthful — but it is still a crash, not a
+   degradation to a host image. **Repairing it requires editing common/common.cpp, which is outside
+   the ownership granted for this phase. I did not edit it. Requesting the grant.** The intended
+   repair is small and has a precedent in the same file: `load_tgt` aborts while `try_load_dft`
+   (common.cpp:2453-2466) returns bool for callers that can recover; `update_tgt`/`update_dft` need
+   the same bool-returning form, and `create_checkpoint` needs to drop the entry (or fall back to a
+   host image) when it returns false.
+2. **Transient peak device memory rises during a replacement.** Preserving the previous checkpoint
+   means the old buffer is still held while the new one is allocated, so a replacing save now peaks
+   at old + new instead of new alone. This is the plan's explicit requirement (phase 1 item 2,
+   "Preserve the previous valid checkpoint until replacement fully succeeds"), and the alternative
+   — release the old buffer, retry the allocation once, accept losing the old image — was
+   deliberately NOT implemented. HQ should decide knowingly. Note the same-byte-total path
+   (`COMMIT_REUSE`) still allocates zero extra bytes, which is the common in-place refresh.
+3. **A dropped entry does not crash the restore path.** `llama_io_read_device::commit` uses
+   `mbufs.at(buft)` (src/llama-context.cpp:3516) inside the `state_seq_set_data` try block
+   (:3724 onward), so a missing entry raises `std::out_of_range`, is caught, and returns 0. A
+   restore of a checkpoint whose in-place save was damaged therefore fails cleanly rather than
+   reading a half-written image. It does not reach the `GGML_ABORT` size-mismatch branch.
+4. **The injected-failure legs need no Vulkan device and no 27B model.** They run against whatever
+   buffer type the test model's KV cache lives in, including plain CPU, so HQ can validate this
+   phase on the generated dummy models with no quiet window and no served chain.
+
+## Test seams
+
+Test 11 extends the EXISTING `tests/test-save-load-state.cpp` fixture (the seam that already drives
+real `llama_state_seq_get_data_ext` / `set_data_ext` device saves; Tests 9 and 10 are its device
+storage-id neighbours). `tests/test-ctx-checkpoint-storage.cpp` was inspected and is NOT the right
+seam: it is a pure host simulation of the server ring allocator and never constructs a context or a
+device buffer. No new test subsystem was added.
